<TopicPage slug="subjects/animation/foundational/the-twelve-principles-of-animation">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the beginner lesson of Animation. You need no prior knowledge — no drawing
    course, no software, no physics. If you have ever drawn a stick figure in the corner
    of a notebook, you have already started. Every technical word is defined the moment
    it appears.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what the twelve principles are, where they came from, and why they survived the jump from pencils to computers;</li>
    <li>use squash and stretch to give a drawing weight, while keeping its volume constant;</li>
    <li>use anticipation to make a fast action readable to the eye;</li>
    <li>control any motion with timing and spacing — the two dials underneath every other principle;</li>
    <li>apply follow-through and overlapping action, and name the remaining principles in one honest sentence each.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Take your time. Each paragraph carries one idea, and each principle is introduced by
    the problem it solves.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with a flipbook</h2>
  <p>
    Draw a stick figure on the last page of a notebook. Draw it again on the page above,
    with one arm moved slightly. Repeat for twenty pages, flick the pages with your
    thumb, and the figure seems to wave. Nothing in the notebook ever moved — each page
    is a still drawing — yet your eye invented the motion across the gaps. That gap is
    the whole art form. Each still drawing is called a <strong>frame</strong>, and film
    shows 24 of them every second, written as 24 frames per second (fps).
  </p>
  <p>
    Animation is the art of the interval. Between one frame and the next there is
    nothing — no motion, no light, no event — but the audience's eye crosses the gap and
    believes. The animator's job is to control what the eye believes: weight, force,
    mood and intention must be smuggled into a handful of stills. Everything the craft
    knows about this was codified by two Disney animators, Ollie Johnston and Frank
    Thomas, in their book <em>The Illusion of Life</em> (1981), as the
    <strong>twelve principles</strong>. The list grew out of the studio's 1930s
    breakthroughs — the years when Mickey Mouse shorts grew into <em>Snow White</em>
    (1937) — and animators worked out, largely by trial and catastrophic error, why some
    movement read as living and some as flickering cardboard.
  </p>
  <p>
    The twelve are best understood as physics and psychology fused. Half the list
    stylises real mechanics — inertia, arcs, momentum — until they read at speed. The
    other half controls attention and feeling: staging, appeal, exaggeration. That
    double parentage is why the list survived the jump from pencils to pixels
    essentially unchanged. The tools were reinvented; the human eye doing the believing
    was not.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> animation is stillness crossed by a believing eye — 24
    frames a second — and the twelve principles are the craft's tested rules for
    controlling that belief. Here is the whole list in order; the rest of the lesson
    walks through it in small passes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="all twelve, in their canonical order" widget="equationStep" config={twelveCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> click through all twelve in order and sort them into
    two families as you go — those that stylise real mechanics (squash and stretch,
    follow-through, slow in and slow out, arcs, timing) and those that direct attention
    and feeling (staging, secondary action, exaggeration, appeal). Notice how each note
    names the problem its principle solves before the technique. Squash and stretch,
    anticipation and timing are the three the rest of this lesson walks through in detail.
  </p>

  <h2>Squash and stretch</h2>
  <p>
    The first principle solves the first problem: how does a drawing — flat, weightless,
    ink — seem to have mass? The answer is to let it deform, the way real things deform,
    while keeping its apparent <strong>volume</strong> (the amount of space it seems to
    fill) constant. A ball drops and elongates with the fall, flattens into a pancake on
    impact, and elongates again as it rebounds. A face turning sharply stretches along
    the turn.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does that read as weight? Because the eye interprets the flexing as the
    <em>effect of forces</em> — the squash is the impact, the stretch is the
    acceleration. The rule that total volume stays constant is what keeps the effect
    physical rather than a rubbery accident: if the ball flattens, it must also widen.
    Squash and stretch is also the craft's forgiveness. Because the eye accepts
    constant-volume deformation as natural, the animator can smear and distort a single
    frame to bridge impossibly fast action — the famous "smear" frame — and the audience
    reads speed and impact rather than breakage.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> squash and stretch is cartoon exaggeration, optional
    outside comedy. It is the opposite — the principle is load-bearing in realistic
    work too, just at subtler amplitude: a cg creature's cheeks and muscle shifting
    minutely with acceleration is the same principle at small scale, and turning it off
    is one of the fastest ways to make expensive animation look like a puppet show.
    The principle scales; only its amplitude changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Anticipation</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Anticipation</strong> moves the audience <em>backwards before forwards</em>:
    the crouch before the jump, the wind-up before the punch, the look before the run.
    It does two jobs at once. Physically, it sells the action — real movement requires
    loading, and a jump launched from standing looks weightless because it skipped the
    load. Communicatively, it directs the eye: the wind-up is a promise that tells the
    viewer what to watch and when.
  </p>
  <p>
    That second job is the only reason fast actions are watchable at all. An action of
    ten frames or fewer is over in under half a second; if the eye did not see it
    coming, it cannot parse what happened. Even reversals get miniature anticipations —
    the head dips for one frame before snapping the other way. The rule of thumb is
    proportional: bigger action, bigger wind-up. Watch a great animator's take-off and
    you can see the entire gag announced a half-second early. Watch a novice's and the
    action is over before the eye arrived.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> anticipation slows the action down, so fast action should skip it. The working rule is the opposite: the faster the action, the more it needs a wind-up, because below about ten frames the eye cannot parse what it did not see coming. Anticipation does not spend the audience's patience — it spends frames to buy legibility, and the proportional rule (bigger action, bigger wind-up) is how animators keep speed and clarity at once.</p>

  <h2>Timing and spacing</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Timing</strong> is how many frames an action takes.
    <strong>Spacing</strong> is where the drawings sit between the first and the last.
    Together they are the deepest pair on the list, because they are the two dials every
    other principle ultimately turns.
  </p>
  <p>
    Timing is characterisation. At 24 fps — usually drawn "on twos", meaning each
    drawing is held for two frames — a heavy body takes more frames to start moving and
    more to stop; a feather takes few frames to reverse. The difference between a sneeze
    and a sigh is the frame count. Mood — nervous, stately, frantic — lives almost
    entirely in how long things are allowed to take.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spacing is subtler, and it is where beginners are actually made.
    <em>Beginners move things; animators space them.</em> A ball dropped with evenly
    spaced drawings falls like a spreadsheet. Space the drawings far apart mid-fall and
    bunched together near impact — the rule called <strong>slow in and slow out</strong>,
    many drawings at the ends of the action, few in the middle — and the same positions
    acquire gravity. Where the drawings cluster, the motion lingers; where they starve,
    it races. In computer animation the same idea arrives as the <em>easing curve</em>:
    the graph that controls how motion flows between keyframes is literally a spacing
    chart. This is why spacing is the first thing any animator learns twice — once in
    pencils, once in graphs.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example: a ball bouncing once. The <strong>keys</strong> — the few drawings
    that define the action — are contact, peak, contact. The peak is where the ball is
    slowest, so drawings cluster there (slow in, slow out). The fall accelerates, so
    drawings space ever farther apart. Contact gets a squash-and-stretch pancake. And
    the whole travel rides a curve, not a straight line — the arc principle. Now compare
    an evenly spaced version with the same three key positions. One feels like a rubber
    ball; the other feels like a pebble tied to a spreadsheet. Nothing about the "what"
    changed; everything about the "when and how spread" did.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> smoother animation means more frames — shoot on ones and the motion is fixed. Frame rate is the least of it: classic Disney shorts were shot on twos (each drawing held for two frames) and still out-feel most amateur work shot on ones, because what the eye reads is spacing, not sampling density. Evenly spaced drawings at any frame rate fall like a spreadsheet; the craft lives in where the drawings sit between the keys.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> squash and stretch fakes weight by deforming at constant
    volume; anticipation buys legibility by winding up; timing and spacing are the two
    dials that give motion its mass. Next: what happens after the main move ends.
  </p>

  <h2>Follow-through and overlapping action</h2>
  <p>
    The continuation principles answer the next problem: motion does not end when the
    pose ends. <strong>Follow-through</strong> says that loosely attached parts — hair,
    cloth, tails, ears, jowls — keep moving after the body stops. They swing past their
    resting position and settle back on their own timing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Overlapping action</strong> goes further: the parts never move in lockstep
    at all. The arm follows the shoulder after a delay, the fingers follow the arm, each
    joint starting and finishing its motion a little later — "successive breaking of
    joints", in the classic phrase. Together the two replace the robot's simultaneity
    with the cascade real bodies actually perform. A walk with dragging weight feels
    alive; the same walk with every limb arriving on the same frame reads as marionette.
  </p>
  <p>
    The principle has a modern descendant worth knowing: the <em>moving hold</em>. A
    character holds a pose while breathing, micro-drifting, hair still swaying — because
    a dead stop, animators learned, reads as death.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> nothing stops at once. Loosely attached parts overrun
    (follow-through), and no two parts move on exactly the same clock (overlapping
    action). The remaining principles fit in one honest paragraph each.
  </p>

  <h2>The remaining principles, honestly summarised</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Staging</em> is the film-director's principle: one clear idea per shot, poses
    readable in silhouette, the eye told exactly where to look. <em>Straight ahead
    versus pose to pose</em> names the two working methods: drawing every frame in
    sequence gives fluid, unpredictable energy, while setting strong keys and
    <strong>inbetweening</strong> — filling in the drawings between the keys — gives
    control and structure. Professionals mix both. <em>Arcs</em>: living things move on
    curves — watch your own hand reach for a cup — and straightening the arc makes
    motion read mechanical, which is precisely why it is used for robots.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Secondary action</em> is supporting motion that enriches without competing — the
    nervous hand, the swinging bag, the raised brow. <em>Exaggeration</em> exists
    because reality is too subtle for 24 frames per second: truthful movement must be
    pushed until it reads, and the paradox is that exaggeration of the true feels
    <em>more</em> real, not less. <em>Solid drawing</em> means every pose must hold
    weight, volume and dimension even as a still — animation is built from frozen
    drawings, so each one is a sculpture. And <em>appeal</em>: the audience must want to
    watch — charisma in design and in motion, for heroes, villains and brooms alike. It
    is the principle that quietly runs the industry.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A thrown punch lasts eight frames. Why does it need anticipation, and what does the animator add?</li>
    <li>Two bouncing balls pass through the same three key positions. Why can one read as rubber and the other as stone?</li>
    <li>A character stops walking but her ponytail keeps swinging and settles back. Which principle is at work, and what is it for?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) Below about ten frames the eye cannot parse an action
    it did not see coming, so the animator adds a wind-up — a one- or two-frame dip —
    to tell the eye where and when to look. (2) Spacing: where the drawings sit between
    the keys decides whether the motion lingers and springs (rubber) or plummets
    evenly (stone-like). (3) Follow-through — loosely attached parts keep moving after
    the body stops, which sells the weight of both the body and the hair.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Animation is the art of the interval: still frames crossed by a believing eye at 24 frames per second.</li>
    <li>The twelve principles were codified by Johnston and Thomas in <em>The Illusion of Life</em> (1981) from Disney's 1930s trial and error.</li>
    <li>Squash and stretch sells weight by deforming at constant volume — it is load-bearing in realistic work too, at subtler amplitude.</li>
    <li>Anticipation moves backwards before forwards; the faster the action, the more it needs a wind-up.</li>
    <li>Timing (frame count) and spacing (where drawings sit between keys) are the two dials underneath every other principle.</li>
    <li>Follow-through and overlapping action keep loosely attached parts moving on their own clocks — nothing stops at once.</li>
    <li>Staging, secondary action, exaggeration, solid drawing and appeal direct the eye and make the audience want to watch.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The list generalises far beyond pencil and paper: the deeper section below follows the
    principles into computer keyframes and easing curves, game state machines, interface
    motion and motion capture. The practical next step is the exercise almost every
    student animates first — the bouncing ball, six of the twelve principles compressed
    into half a second.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The twelve generalise beyond their birthplace, and each generalisation is a test of
    what the principle really was. In computer animation, the hand-drawn craft became
    software machinery: keys and inbetweens became keyframes and interpolation, spacing
    charts became easing curves, and rigs now automate squash-and-stretch and
    follow-through. But the animator's judgement moved rather than vanished, because
    the default setting — even interpolation — produces exactly the even spacing the
    principles exist to defeat. In game animation there are no fixed frames at all:
    motion is state-machine logic and blend trees, and the principles become the
    reasons transitions are weighted, animations buffered, and "anticipation frames"
    preserved even at the cost of input responsiveness. Feel is bought with
    physics-flavoured lies. Interaction designers discovered the list independently:
    interface motion that eases in and out, anticipates, and follows through simply
    feels better, and the platforms' motion guidelines read like an eight-principle
    simplification of Johnston and Thomas.
  </p>
  <p>
    The open questions are where the craft is currently arguing with itself. How much
    of the list survives motion capture, when the "performance" is recorded rather than
    authored? Enough that capturing is the easy half — the principles return at
    clean-up, where captured timing gets re-timed and captured arcs get re-curved. How
    far can simulation carry weight and cloth before the animator's lie is better than
    the physics' truth? The physics is believable but not necessarily
    <em>readable</em> — and readability was always the point. And machine-learning
    inbetweening raises the profession's sharpest question: interpolation is exactly
    what models do well, yet the spacing decisions that make it art are the ones the
    animator owned. The principles were extracted from four decades of trial and error
    in the 1930s. Nothing so far — pencils, pixels, capture or models — has found a way
    to throw them out.
  </p>
  <p>
    The most durable way to internalise the list is the animator's own practice
    exercise, the one page almost every student animates first: the bouncing ball. It
    compresses six of the twelve principles into half a second — squash and stretch at
    the contact, slow in and slow out at the peak, the arc of the travel, timing for
    weight, spacing for momentum. A student can draw it a hundred times, changing one
    principle at a time, until the relationship between the dials and the feeling is
    not knowledge but reflex. Studios still assign it, and the craft's most-used
    handbook builds its entire opening from it. To outsiders the principles are a
    checklist; to practitioners they are closer to the fingerings of an instrument —
    twelve habits drilled until the illusion of life is something the hands can do
    without supervision. Watched in a master's shot, they dissolve: you see a character
    being alive, not twelve rules being obeyed.
  </p>

